## Validation mechanisms

`tests/check.py` drives the public socket protocol and uses only Python's standard
library. Native equivalence calls the supplied ABI v1 directly via ctypes.
Database inspection opens the designated dynamic RocksDB C API read-only after
stopping the writer. It verifies exact key membership, contiguous receipt/pending
partitions, ID bijection, cancellation references, digests, and byte/job counters.
Seeded recovery/corruption fixtures are written only while the service is stopped.
Crashes use SIGKILL without service hooks or native interposition. Tests retain
acknowledged admissions and cancellation evidence across restarts.

`readelf` confirms ELF64/AArch64, dynamic dependency on `/adapter/librosetta.so`
and `librocksdb.so.7.8`, and the adapter runtime path. `ldd` resolves those pinned
libraries. Final source, executable, and document hashes are in `SHA256SUMS`.

## Evidence limits and interpretation

- No externally interposed entrypoint/write barriers were supplied or added.
  The contract's exact barrier/crash/concurrency qualification remains external.
- ReleaseSafe bounds/overflow checks are enabled. No native ASan, whole-service
  race detector, or allocator-failure injection was run. No equivalence to Go
  race checking, exhaustive race proof, or power-loss guarantee is claimed.
- Actual storage write errors and native allocation failures were not injected.
  Both error paths stop before acknowledging/terminalizing failed transitions.
- Count backpressure was observed. Byte-accounting invariants and aggregate
  request rejection were tested, but simultaneous admitted-byte saturation was
  not artificially held in place.
- The contract freeze manifest was absent; preparation prose was treated as
  context under the user's explicit implementation-phase authorization.
- Ambiguous wire details and chosen behavior are documented in HANDOFF.md.
- Host Python emits sandbox/xcrun cache warnings while launching the broker.
  Broker commands completed despite those warnings; no host build was performed.
